# TCP vs UDP: Key Differences and When to Use Each

## Core Differences

| Aspect | TCP | UDP |
|--------|-----|-----|
| **Connection** | Connection-oriented (3-way handshake) | Connectionless |
| **Reliability** | Guaranteed delivery, ordering, and integrity | Best-effort; no guarantees |
| **Header size** | 20–60 bytes | 8 bytes |
| **Speed** | Slower (overhead from ACKs, retransmits) | Faster (minimal overhead) |
| **Flow/Congestion control** | Yes (windowing, slow start, etc.) | No |
| **Ordering** | Preserved | Not preserved |
| **Error checking** | Checksum + retransmission | Checksum only (no retransmit) |
| **Broadcast/Multicast** | Not supported | Supported |
| **Data stream** | Byte stream | Datagram (message boundaries preserved) |

## How They Work

**TCP** establishes a connection via a three-way handshake (SYN → SYN-ACK → ACK), numbers every segment, acknowledges receipt, retransmits lost packets, and reorders out-of-order data. It also adapts its sending rate to network conditions (congestion control).

**UDP** just fires datagrams at the destination. No handshake, no acknowledgments, no retransmission. If a packet is lost or arrives out
